ot\n    been infected with the bacterial pathogen and who does not have TB.\n\n\n\n\n\n                                          Fig. 4.1\n\n    Blood vessels and some structures of the gas exchange system are visible in Fig. 4.1.\n\n   On Fig. 4.1, use a label line and label:\n\n     •   a bronchus\n     •   a bronchiole\n     •   a blood vessel.\n                                                                                                                 [3]\n\n(e)  In some people with TB, areas known as granulomas may form in lung tissue as part of an\n          immune response to the pathogen.\n\n              Fig. 4.2 is a photomicrograph of a granuloma in lung tissue.\n\n\n                                                                           multinucleated\n                                                                                giant cells,\n                                                                   formed by the\ndarkly staining\n                                                                              fusion of many\nwhite blood cells\n                                                               macrophages\nin outer area of\ngranuloma\n\n                                                                                   granuloma\n                                                              dead cells\n                                                              and infected\n                                                                                       cells (mainly\n                                                                 macrophages)\n\n\n\n\n\n                                                  Fig. 4.2\n\n           With reference to Fig. 4.1 and Fig. 4.2, describe and explain how the changes that occur as a\n              result of granuloma formation:\n\n               •   can affect gas exchange and harm the health of an infected person\n               •   may help to prevent TB developing in other parts 