   "search_text": "6   Horseradish peroxidase (HRP) is an enzyme that can be extracted from the roots of the horseradish\n     plant, Armoracia rusticana. HRP is used extensively in industry and technology.\n\n     In the reaction catalysed by HRP, hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) is used to oxidise an organic\n     substrate. This is summarised in Fig. 6.1.\n\n                                  HRP\n           organic substrate + hydrogen peroxide              oxidised substrate + water\n\n                                               Fig. 6.1\n\n     (a)  Inhibitors can have an  effect on Vmax , the maximum  rate  of  reaction, and Km , the\n         Michaelis –Menten constant, of HRP.\n\n          Fig. 6.2 shows the effect of substrate concentration on the rate of reaction of HRP.\n\n                              8\n\n\n                              6\n                                  initial rate\n                           of reaction\n                              4\n                                        / arbitrary\n                          units\n                              2\n\n\n                              0\n                                     0.0        0.1        0.2        0.3\n                                       substrate concentration / mmol dm–3\n\n\n                                               Fig. 6.2\n\n        Complete Fig. 6.2 by drawing a curve to show how the presence of a non‑competitive inhibitor\n            will affect the rate of reaction of HRP.\n\n       Use the curve you have drawn to obtain an estimate of Km.\n\n                                       Km =  .........................................................  [2]\n\n     (b)  Scientists can design synthetic DNA nucleotide sequences to produce a synthetic HRP gene.\n       These sequences will include a start codon and a stop codon so that translation of messenger\n      RNA (mRNA) can occur.\n\n         Explain what is meant by a start codon and a stop codon.\n\n               ...................................................................................................................................................\n\n               ...................................................................................................................................................\n\n               ...................................................................................................................................................\n\n               .............................................................................................................................................  [2]\n\n(c) HRP is used in an immunological test known as a sandwich ELISA. One use of the test is to\n     diagnose disease.\n\n      Fig. 6.3 outlines the main steps in a sandwich ELISA in which a toxin released by a pathogen\n       is detected in a sample of body fluid taken from a person who is ill.\n\n    The test involves two types of monoclonal antibody that can bind to the toxin, a capture\n     antibody and a detection antibody.\n\n\n\n                                                                                         test well\nstep 1  Prepare a test well by immobilising\n         the capture monoclonal antibody to\n                                                                           capture\n         the support surface.\n                                                                            antibody\n\n\n\nstep 2  Remove a sample of the body fluid from the person who is ill and add the sample to\n         the test well.\n\n\n\nstep 3  Wash out the test well to remove any\n        substances not bound to the capture                                     toxin\n         antibody.\n\n\n                                                   HRP\n\n\n                                                                                detection\n                                                                            antibody\n\nstep 4  Add detection antibody to the test.\n                                                                                     toxin\n\n\n\n\nstep 5  Wash out the test well to remove any detection antibody that is not bound to the toxin.\n\n\n\nstep 6  Add substrates for catalysis by HRP and add acid after a set time to stop the reaction.\n\n\n\n                                           Fig. 6.3\n\n        (i)  Complete Fig. 6.4 to show the situation in the test well after step 5.\n\n\n\n\n\n                                           Fig. 6.4\n                                                                                                                  [1]\n\n(ii)  With reference to Fig. 6.3, describe one difference between the capture antibody and\n     the detection antibody.\n\n          ...........................................................................................................................................\n\n          ...........................................................................................................................................\n\n          ...........................................................................................................................................\n\n          .....................................................................................................................................  [1]\n\n(iii)  Fig. 6.5 outlines the reaction catalysed by HRP in step 6. The organic substrate, TMB, is\n       in excess and changes colour when it is oxidised. This indicates a positive test result.\n\n                        HRP\n          TMB   + H2O2                oxidised TMB + water\n             colourless                         blue‑green\n\n                                       Fig. 6.5\n\n    A student suggested that:\n\n       •   a low concentration of toxin may by diagnosed as a negative result instead of a\n           positive result\n       •    using a colorimeter after step 6 in the sandwich ELISA test would provide a\n           quantitative result and help to avoid this error.\n\n     With reference to Fig. 6.5, explain why using a colorimeter would provide a quantitative\n     measurement for detection of a low concentration of the toxin and help to confirm a\n      positive result.\n\n          ...........................................................................................................................................\n\n          ...........................................................................................................................................\n\n          ...........................................................................................................................................\n\n          ...........................................................................................................................................\n\n          ...........................................................................................................................................\n\n          ...........................................................................................................................................\n\n          ...........................................................................................................................................\n\n          ...........................................................................................................................................\n\n          .....................................................................................................................................  [4]\n\n                                                                                                 [Total: 10]\n",
